

DHCLIENT(8)         UNIX Programmer's Manual         DHCLIENT(8)



NAME
     dhclient - Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol client

SYNOPSIS
     dhclient [-d domain] [-n nameserver] [iface]

DESCRIPTION
     Dhclient configures a network interface from a DHCP server: it
     runs the DISCOVER/OFFER/REQUEST/ACK exchange (with retransmit
     and backoff), assigns the leased address, netmask, broadcast,
     and gateway, and writes the resolver configuration to
     /etc/resolv.conf.  With no iface it configures the primary
     (first) interface.

     The lease is remembered in /etc/dhcp.lease; on the next boot
     dhclient re-requests that same address (INIT-REBOOT) before
     falling back to a fresh DISCOVER.  There is no background renew
     daemon; re-run dhclient to renew.

     The DNS server and search domain come from the DHCP reply
     (options 6 and 15) by default.  They may be overridden, in
     increasing order of precedence:

     /etc/dhclient.conf
          A line `domain name' (or `search name') sets the search
          domain; a line `nameserver a.b.c.d' sets the DNS server.
          Blank lines and `#' comments are ignored.

     -d domain
          Override the search domain.

     -n nameserver
          Override the DNS server IP address.

     An override is useful when the LAN's DHCP server sends no
     search domain, or a better nameserver is known locally.

FILES
     /etc/resolv.conf    resolver configuration (written)
     /etc/dhcp.lease     remembered lease address
     /etc/dhclient.conf  local resolver overrides (read)

SEE ALSO
     host(1), ifconfig(8), resolv.conf(5)

BUGS
     Only the first nameserver and a single search domain are
     honored, matching the slim FGX resolver.  DHCP option 119 (the
     domain search list) is not parsed; use option 15, the conf
     file, or -d.



